.TH MU2E-POWER-ON 1 "2026-09-18" "mu2edaq-power-recovery 0.1.0" "Mu2e DAQ Operations"
.SH NAME
mu2e-power-on \- phase 2: bring the Mu2e DAQ cluster up in dependency order
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B mu2e-power-on
.RB [ \-\-execute ]
.RB [ \-\-from
.IR STAGE ]
.RB [ \-\-until
.IR STAGE ]
.RI [ options ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
Runs phase 2 of
.BR mu2e-power-recovery (1):
the power-on sequence defined in
.BR mu2edaq-power-sequence.yaml (5).
.PP
For each stage, in order:
.IP \(bu 2
read each node's chassis power state over IPMI;
.IP \(bu 2
issue
.B chassis power on
to whatever is off;
.IP \(bu 2
wait for SSH to answer, up to the stage's
.IR boot_timeout ;
.IP \(bu 2
wait the stage's
.I settle
period, so late-starting units are not sampled mid-start;
.IP \(bu 2
run the stage's check profile;
.IP \(bu 2
decide whether the stage met its
.I require
setting
.RB ( all ", " majority " or " any ).
.PP
The shipped order is gateways, then
.IR mu2e-mgr-01 ,
then the data loggers, the DCS servers, the CFO, and finally the detector
readout nodes. It is operational rather than arbitrary:
.I mu2e-mgr-01
exports
.I /home
and the shared areas, so every later node's mounts fail if it is not healthy
first; the CFO distributes the clock and control fabric the readout nodes
synchronise to.
.PP
A stage that does not meet its requirement stops the sequence, unless
.B \-\-continue\-on\-error
is given. That is deliberate: later stages depend on the services the earlier
ones provide, so continuing would produce failures that say nothing new.
.SH SAFETY
This command is a
.B dry run
unless
.B \-\-execute
is given: power states are read, and the report shows what
.I would
have been switched on, but no chassis is touched.
.PP
.BR "chassis power off" ", " cycle " and " reset
are refused for the hosts listed under
.I protected:
in
.BR mu2edaq-topology.yaml (5)
\(em the gateways,
.I mu2e-mgr-01
and
.I mu2e-dcs-01
\(em regardless of
.BR \-\-execute .
The gateway stage never issues a power command at all; it verifies only.
.PP
All the BMCs share one credential set, so a refused BMC credential is not
retried and not repeated: the first rejection stops the run issuing IPMI and
produces one diagnosis naming the username that was refused, instead of walking
the same wrong credential through every chassis and locking the account on
each. Only a RAKP failure or
.I "unauthorized name"
counts;
.I "Unable to establish IPMI v2 / RMCP+ session"
does not, because that is also what a chassis with no standby power says, and
after an outage that is the expected case. Set
.I ipmi.stop_on_auth_failure: false
to override.
.PP
Every attempt, including every refusal, is recorded in the run store before the
command is issued, so a tool that dies mid-action still leaves evidence of what
it was doing.
.SH OPTIONS
Every option of
.BR mu2e-power-recovery (1)
is accepted except
.BR \-\-phase ,
which is fixed to
.BR poweron .
.SS Sequence control
.TP
.BI \-\-from " STAGE"
Start at this stage. Use it to resume after fixing whatever stopped a previous
attempt.
.TP
.BI \-\-until " STAGE"
Stop after this stage. Useful for bringing up the infrastructure nodes and
pausing before the detector readout.
.TP
.B "Spelling, for both of the above"
A stage name that matches nothing is
.I silently ignored,
not rejected:
.B \-\-from
falls back to the first stage and
.B \-\-until
to the last. So
.B "mu2e-power-on \-\-execute \-\-from manger"
\(em one letter wrong \(em powers on the
.I whole
sequence, readout included, with no error and no note in the report. For a flag
whose purpose is to bound what gets switched on, check the name took: the stage
names are the
.I name:
keys in
.BR mu2edaq-power-sequence.yaml (5)
.RB ( gateways ", " manager ", " dataloggers ", " dcs ", " cfo ", " readout ),
and a dry run without
.B \-\-execute
lists the stages it would act on.
.TP
.B \-\-continue\-on\-error
Do not stop at the first stage that fails its requirement.
.SS Run control
.TP
.BI \-\-label " TEXT"
Label for this run, shown in the report header and the logbook subject.
.TP
.B \-\-execute
Required for this phase to switch anything on. Without it nothing is issued.
.TP
.B \-\-simulate
Contact nothing at all; answer every command from the built-in script. It
overrides
.B \-\-execute
on the same command line, so a rehearsal cannot become a real power-on by
leaving a flag behind.
.TP
.BI \-\-location " NAME"
Repeatable. Accepted, but it does not narrow this phase: each stage in
.BR mu2edaq-power-sequence.yaml (5)
carries its own
.I location:
and its node list is expanded against that.
.TP
.BI \-\-node " HOST"
Repeatable. Accepted, and likewise does not narrow this phase \(em stage
membership comes from the sequence file, not from the command line. To bound
what is powered on, use
.BR \-\-from " and " \-\-until ,
or edit the sequence file.
.TP
.B \-\-include\-failed
Accepted; it governs phase 3 and has no effect here.
.SS Credentials
.TP
.BI \-\-principal " PRINCIPAL"
Kerberos principal for ordinary logins, used for the post-boot checks and for
reaching the gateway that runs
.BR ipmitool (1).
.TP
.BI \-\-root\-principal " PRINCIPAL"
Kerberos principal with root access on the nodes, for the checks that need a
root session.
.TP
.B \-\-no\-prompt
Never prompt for a password; fail with instructions instead.
.TP
.BI \-\-vault\-addr " URL"
HashiCorp Vault address. The BMC credentials come from it, so this phase cannot
run without either Vault or the configured file fallback.
.SS Report
.TP
.BI \-\-output\-dir " DIR"
Where the report pages are written. Default:
.IR html/ .
.TP
.B \-\-publish
Copy the report to
.I report.publish.target
afterwards.
.TP
.BI \-\-publish\-target " DEST"
Override the publication target. Implies
.BR \-\-publish .
.TP
.B \-\-no\-report
Do not write the HTML pages; console output only.
.TP
.B \-\-post\-ecl
Accepted; the logbook entry is written by phase 4. See
.BR mu2e-power-report (1).
.TP
.BI \-\-run\-id " N"
Accepted; only phase 4 reads it.
.SS General
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Print the option summary and exit.
.TP
.B \-\-version
Print
.I mu2edaq-power-recovery 0.1.0
and exit.
.TP
.BI \-\-config " FILE"
Main configuration file. Default:
.IR config/power-recovery.yaml .
.TP
.BI \-\-env\-file " FILE"
Dotenv file. Default:
.IR config/.env .
.TP
.BI \-\-database\-url " URL"
SQLAlchemy URL for the run store.
.TP
.B \-\-no\-self\-update
Skip the phase-0 check for a newer revision.
.TP
.B \-\-list\-checks
Print every registered check and exit.
.TP
.B \-\-list\-nodes
Print the node inventory and exit.
.TP
.B \-\-json
Print the result as JSON on stdout instead of tables.
.TP
.BR \-v ", " \-\-verbose
Debug logging, including the exact
.B ipmitool
invocation and one line per credential attempted.
.TP
.BR \-q ", " \-\-quiet
Warnings and errors only.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Every stage met its requirement.
.TP
.B 1
The sequence ran but a stage failed, or nodes failed their checks.
.TP
.B 2
The sequence could not start: no gateway, or no BMC credentials.
.SH EXAMPLES
See what would happen, without doing it:
.RS
.EX
mu2e-power-on
.EE
.RE
.PP
Bring up the infrastructure only, then stop and look:
.RS
.EX
mu2e-power-on \-\-execute \-\-until cfo
.EE
.RE
.PP
Resume at the DCS stage after repairing it:
.RS
.EX
mu2e-power-on \-\-execute \-\-from dcs
.EE
.RE
.PP
Rehearse the sequence with nothing attached:
.RS
.EX
mu2e-power-on \-\-simulate \-v
.EE
.RE
